The training data exhibit a clear strength-ductility Pareto surface whose extremes and knee points are produced by distinct, readily recognizable metallurgical families:

- solution-treated 18Cr–8/12Ni austenitics (fast or moderate cool, little or no aging) give the high-elongation end (~240 MPa / 58–70 %);
- Nb-stabilized 16Cr–15Ni–Mo austenitics give an intermediate knee (~520 MPa / 37 %);
- 21Cr–13Ni–Si or lean-duplex grades occupy the 350–410 MPa / 40–53 % region;
- 12Cr–8Ni–8Mn–V/Nb precipitation-hardened grades sit at ~700 MPa / 25 %;
- low-temper 11–13 Cr martensites (especially the 0.13 C–1.8 W–V composition tempered near 450 °C) reach ~1300–1380 MPa while still retaining ~15 % elongation.

Exact or near-exact feature matches of these five families exist in the candidate pool and are therefore expected to land on the same non-dominated surface:

- c002 reproduces the 18Cr–12Ni austenitic (aging 700 °C / 10 kh versus the training 750 °C / 3 kh) \(\to\) ~250 MPa / 55–58 %;
- c016 reproduces the 20.5Cr–13.5Ni–2.5Si grade \(\to\) ~375 MPa / 53 %;
- c078 is compositionally identical to the Nb-bearing 16Cr–15Ni alloy (anneal only 20 °C lower) \(\to\) ~520 MPa / 37 %;
- c063 reproduces the 12Cr–8Ni–8Mn PH steel (aging 550 °C / 10 kh versus training 600 °C / 5 kh) \(\to\) ~650–700 MPa / 22–25 %;
- c053 is compositionally almost identical to the 11 Cr martensite tempered at 450 °C \(\to\) ~1350 MPa / 15 %.

The five predicted points are mutually non-dominated and are spaced along the entire front; their hypervolume in the normalized (YS, elongation) plane is therefore maximal.

Final: c002, c016, c053, c063, c078
